# (id, name, latin, region, location, description, function, clinical, attachment-from, attachment-to)
FIB=[
('patellar-ligament','Patellar ligament','Ligamentum patellae','lower-limb','Front of the knee, between the kneecap and the shin bone.','A strong flat white band that is the continuation of the quadriceps tendon below the patella.','Transmits the pull of the quadriceps to the tibia, so the knee can straighten. It is tapped with a hammer in the knee-jerk reflex (L3, L4).','Rupture or tendinopathy ("jumper\'s knee") weakens or stops straightening of the knee. Surgeons often use its central third as a graft to rebuild a torn anterior cruciate ligament.','Apex (lower tip) of the patella.','Tibial tuberosity.'),
('medial-collateral-ligament','Medial collateral ligament of the knee','Ligamentum collaterale tibiale','lower-limb','Inner side of the knee.','A broad flat band on the inner side of the knee that blends with the medial meniscus.','Stops the knee from bending inward (valgus stress) and steadies it when it is straight.','The most commonly injured knee ligament, usually from a blow to the outer side of the knee (a tackle). The terrible triad is a tear of this ligament, the anterior cruciate and the medial meniscus.','Medial epicondyle of the femur.','Medial surface of the upper tibial shaft.'),
('lateral-collateral-ligament','Lateral collateral ligament of the knee','Ligamentum collaterale fibulare','lower-limb','Outer side of the knee.','A rounded cord on the outer side of the knee. It is separate from the joint capsule and the lateral meniscus.','Stops the knee from bending outward (varus stress).','Injured by a blow to the inner side of the knee or by twisting. The common fibular nerve runs close behind it and may be hurt in the same injury.','Lateral epicondyle of the femur.','Head of the fibula.'),
('anterior-cruciate-ligament','Anterior cruciate ligament','Ligamentum cruciatum anterius','lower-limb','Inside the knee joint, in the middle between the femur and the tibia.','A strong band that runs from the back of the femur forward and down to the front of the tibia. Schematic position.','Stops the tibia from sliding forward on the femur and controls twisting. It is a main stabiliser of the knee.','Tears are common in sports with sudden stops, jumps or side-steps (football, basketball, skiing). The knee feels unstable and a positive Lachman or anterior drawer test is found. Reconstruction uses a graft.','Inner side of the lateral femoral condyle (back part).','Front of the intercondylar area of the tibia.'),
('posterior-cruciate-ligament','Posterior cruciate ligament','Ligamentum cruciatum posterius','lower-limb','Inside the knee joint, crossing behind the anterior cruciate.','A thick band that runs from the front of the femoral notch backward and down to the back of the tibia. Schematic position.','Stops the tibia from sliding backward on the femur. It is stronger than the anterior cruciate.','Torn in a hard blow to the front of the shin (a dashboard injury in a car crash) or by overextension. The tibia sags backward (posterior drawer sign).','Outer surface of the medial femoral condyle.','Back of the intercondylar area of the tibia.'),
('iliotibial-tract','Iliotibial tract','Tractus iliotibialis','lower-limb','Outer side of the thigh, from the hip to below the knee.','A thick white band of deep fascia (a long flat tendon) that receives the tensor fasciae latae and most of gluteus maximus.','Steadies the hip and knee when standing and walking and helps hold the knee straight.','Iliotibial band syndrome, a common cause of outer knee pain in runners and cyclists, comes from friction over the lateral femoral epicondyle.','Iliac crest and the tensor fasciae latae and gluteus maximus.','Lateral condyle of the tibia (Gerdy\'s tubercle).'),
('inguinal-ligament','Inguinal ligament','Ligamentum inguinale','pelvis','Crease between the lower abdomen and the thigh.','A band formed by the lower edge of the external oblique aponeurosis. It runs between the hip bone and the pubis.','Forms the floor of the inguinal canal and the roof of the passage to the thigh for the femoral vessels and nerve.','Inguinal hernias push through the inguinal canal above this ligament; femoral hernias come through below it. It is the landmark for locating the femoral artery (mid-inguinal point) and nerve.','Anterior superior iliac spine.','Pubic tubercle.'),
('linea-alba','Linea alba','Linea alba','abdomen','Vertical midline of the abdomen, from the breastbone to the pubis.','A tough white line formed where the aponeuroses of the three flat abdominal muscles meet between the two rectus abdominis muscles.','Joins the right and left sides of the abdominal wall and gives attachment to the abdominal muscles. It has few blood vessels, which makes it a favourite surgical incision.','A midline incision through the linea alba opens the abdomen with little bleeding. Weak spots cause midline hernias (epigastric hernia, incisional hernia). Pregnancy can widen it (diastasis recti).','Xiphoid process and costal margin.','Pubic symphysis.'),
('flexor-retinaculum','Flexor retinaculum of the wrist','Retinaculum flexorum','upper-limb','Front of the wrist, over the carpal bones.','A thick white band that bridges the curve of the carpal bones and turns it into the carpal tunnel.','Holds the finger flexor tendons close to the wrist and forms the roof of the carpal tunnel through which the median nerve passes.','Carpal tunnel syndrome: swelling in the tunnel squeezes the median nerve, causing numbness and tingling of the thumb, index and middle fingers and weak grip. Treatment can include cutting this ligament.','Pisiform and hook of the hamate.','Scaphoid tubercle and trapezium.'),
('interosseous-membrane-forearm','Interosseous membrane of the forearm','Membrana interossea antebrachii','upper-limb','Between the radius and the ulna.','A strong white fibrous sheet that joins the shafts of the two forearm bones.','Transfers force from the hand (radius) to the ulna and the arm, and gives attachment to deep forearm muscles.','If a fall on the outstretched hand fractures the radius, the force passes along this sheet. A tear with a radial head fracture and wrist injury is called an Essex-Lopresti injury.','Interosseous border of the radius.','Interosseous border of the ulna.'),
('interosseous-membrane-leg','Interosseous membrane of the leg','Membrana interossea cruris','lower-limb','Between the tibia and the fibula.','A strong white fibrous sheet joining the shafts of the two leg bones. It has an opening at the top for the anterior tibial vessels.','Binds the leg bones together, passes weight from the tibia to the fibula and gives attachment to deep leg muscles.','Tears in high ankle sprains (syndesmosis injuries) and in tibia and fibula fractures can leave the ankle unstable.','Interosseous border of the tibia.','Interosseous border of the fibula.'),
('long-plantar-ligament','Long plantar ligament','Ligamentum plantare longum','lower-limb','Sole of the foot, along the outer side.','The longest white ligament of the foot. It runs from the heel bone to the cuboid and the base of the outer metatarsals.','Helps hold up the long arch of the foot and bridges the tendon of fibularis longus.','Overstretching helps cause flat foot (fallen arch) and heel and midfoot pain.','Plantar surface of the calcaneus.','Cuboid and bases of metatarsals 2 to 5.'),
]
